meta {
  name: Claim a name
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

put {
  url: {{groupService}}/members
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "groupKey": "{{groupKey}}",
    "memberSecret": "{{memberSecret}}",
    "displayName": "{{displayName}}"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  const CryptoJS = require("crypto-js");
  if (!bru.getEnvVar("memberSecret")) {
    bru.setEnvVar("memberSecret", CryptoJS.lib.WordArray.random(32).toString(CryptoJS.enc.Base64));
  }
}

tests {
  test("answers this member's id, the lease it holds, and the group around it", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.memberId).to.be.a("string");
    expect(res.body.displayName).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("displayName"));
    expect(res.body.leaseSeconds).to.be.greaterThan(0);
    expect(res.body.members.map((m) => m.memberId)).to.include(res.body.memberId);
  });
}

docs {
  # Claim a name

  States this member's presence in the group: the id its secret derives, under the name it goes by.

  The member id is a keyed digest of the member secret under the group key, so the relay lists and logs which member a connection belongs to without carrying the secret it was derived from, and no other member can state this one's presence.

  `members` is every live member of the group, this one included, with `publishing` read off the relay's connection list rather than stated by anybody.

  The lease is `leaseSeconds` long and the app refreshes it on the 2 s relay poll it already runs, so nine missed passes are survivable and a machine that stopped is out of the group inside a moment.

  The group is reconciled before the answer, so a lease that lapsed since the last call has its connections closed by the call that noticed.

  The secret is drawn here where the environment holds none, which is what the app does on its first join to a group.
}
